<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Rebuild the offline book and recognise the old recipe conventions.
</div>

This chapter uses the 24-person synthetic questionnaire test fixture described in [Getting started](book-00-start.html).
**You need:** the packages and the matching practice connection described in
[Before you start](book-00-start.html#before-you-start).
Run the sections in order in one R session. All printed results below were computed
with dsOMOP and dsOMOPClient 2.10.5. They are teaching data, not clinical findings.

Concept selections refer to the searches and saved definitions in
[chapter 4](book-04-vocabulary.html), including the separate questionnaire
catalogue. Additional body-measurement concepts are found on screen in the
[formula walkthrough](book-15-formulas.html#find-the-inputs-before-calculating).

## Start this chapter

Start a fresh R session in the `book-work` directory created in
[Getting started](book-00-start.html#get-the-book-helpers). Run this setup first:

```{r chapter-setup}
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/fixture.R")
practice <- book_fixture()
conns <- practice$conns
names(conns)
```

The `site` connection is ready. This chapter creates its temporary questionnaire fixture locally.

## Source and rendered lessons

The editable chapters are `vignettes/book-*.Rmd.orig`. The precompute
script executes them against the synthetic fixture and writes `.Rmd` with the
actual output embedded. The resulting files contain ordinary Markdown code
fences, so the website does not connect to a database when it builds.

From the book client checkout, run `tools/book/run-pod.sh` in this workspace, or
run `Rscript tools/book/precompute.R` in an R environment containing the matching
packages and fixture dependencies. Then run `Rscript tools/book/build-site.R`
to build the site and its helper downloads, followed by
`Rscript tools/book/verify-bundle.R` to execute every chapter from the final ZIP.
The pod driver is workspace-specific; the R scripts and fixture are portable.

## Record the software versions

```{r versions}
vapply(c("dsOMOP", "dsOMOPClient", "DSI", "DSLite", "dsBase", "dsBaseClient"),
       function(p) as.character(packageVersion(p)), character(1))
```

These are the actual versions used for this rendering. Detailed assertions,
source hashes and test logs are retained under `tools/book/evidence`.

## Reproduce both practice resources

Run `Rscript tools/book/load-practice.R ../practice-data`. The loader pins the
EunomiaDatasets source revision and verifies SHA-256 for both archives. It creates
`gibleed.sqlite`, `numeric.sqlite` and `manifest.json` in `../practice-data`.
If you chose a different loader directory, set `BOOK_PRACTICE_DIR` to that
directory before running the lessons. These are administrator-side
operations on public synthetic data, separate from analyst DataSHIELD calls.
The following table reads your loader's manifest from that same directory.
The checkout's `tools/book/evidence/book2/practice-manifest.json` is historical
build evidence; readers do not need it.

```{r practice-manifest}
practice_dir <- Sys.getenv("BOOK_PRACTICE_DIR", file.path("..", "practice-data"))
manifest <- jsonlite::read_json(file.path(practice_dir, "manifest.json"))
loaded <- do.call(rbind, lapply(manifest, function(x) data.frame(
  Dataset = x$dataset, Measurements = x$measurements$rows,
  Numeric = x$measurements$numeric_values,
  Numeric_with_unit = x$measurements$numeric_with_unit)))
knitr::kable(loaded, row.names = FALSE)
```

GiBleed supplies the basics; Synthea27Nj supplies numeric labs. Its numeric values
and mapped units come from the pinned source, without fabricated lab extensions.
The questionnaire fixture remains separate and is explicitly named in its lessons.

The [loader](../downloads/load-practice.R), [two-resource DSLite helper](../downloads/practice.R)
and [questionnaire fixture](../downloads/fixture.R) are downloadable from this site.
The same SQLite files can be mounted into another Rock server; their resource
registration is shown in chapter 0. Dependencies are listed in `tools/book/README.md`.

## The local test driver

DSLite 1.5 rejects some quoted dsBase call strings containing commas. The practice
helper applies the same scoped lexer repair used by the package's release tests.
It changes tokenisation only: ds.table, assignments and all disclosure checks still
run for real. It does not apply to Opal. The two intentional refusal chunks in chapter 12
omit DSLite’s extra missing-symbol cleanup warnings; they display the actual
policy error, and the verification checks that no refused output exists. The helper also injects a synthetic test
pseudonym root; a deployed server manages its own private root automatically.

## Migrating older recipes

The 2.10.2 colleague example selected a categorical observation with
`omop_variable(table = ..., concept_id = ..., format = "last_value", value_source = ...)`.
Its translation path required an output name ending in `_concept_id`. The death
feature needed `value_source = "cause_concept_id"`; `column` alone did not select
the reduced value. Those are legacy conventions, not the API taught here.

In language 2, use `event = omop_event(...)` and
`reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", value = "value_as_concept_id")`.
Use `format = "wide"` and `grain = "person"` on the output. Concept typing now
supplies labels regardless of the output name. Re-execute an old recipe to obtain
new labelled assignments; renaming an existing symbol does not recompute it.

Calendar migration also matters. `first_date` and `last_date` remain day offsets.
For calendar values use `omop_date()`, for calendar precision use
`omop_date_spec()`, and for completed age at death use `omop_age_at("death")`.
Do not relabel annual year subtraction as completed age.

## Inspect the executed language

```{r migration-check}
check <- omop_recipe(variables = list(age = omop_age_at("death")),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
recipe_explain(check, conns = conns)$selected
print(recipe_execute(check, out = c(people = "D_migration"), conns = conns))
```

If an explanation selects v1, it is explaining the legacy execution path. Merely
printing a forced v2 conversion would not prove that the actual execution uses it.

## Try it

Check that the migration recipe is native before using it.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>


```{r solution}
recipe_explain(check, conns = conns)$selected
```

</details>

## Exercise 2: a common variant

Round-trip a native recipe through JSON.

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

```{r book4-exercise-2}
restored <- recipe_import_json(recipe_export_json(check))
identical(recipe_to_plan(check), recipe_to_plan(restored))
```

TRUE establishes plan equality for this example; retain package and vocabulary versions as well.

</details>

## Exercise 3: a pitfall to avoid

Inspect the loaded manifest dataset names. Does rebuilding the site reload a database?

<details><summary>Solution 3</summary>

```{r book4-exercise-3}
vapply(manifest, function(x) x$dataset, "")
```

No. Loading practice data, precomputing examples and rendering the site are three distinct operations.

</details>
## Strict Circe imports preserve the question

Circe JSON describes an OHDSI cohort. Import is a translation into the executable
recipe subset, not a promise that every Circe setting is supported. Refusing an
unsupported criterion protects the research question from silent simplification.
This deliberately invalid example stops at the unsupported additional criteria:

```{r circe-refusal}
circe_message <- tryCatch(recipe_import_circe(jsonlite::toJSON(list(
  AdditionalCriteria = list(Type = "ALL", CriteriaList = list(list(unsupported = TRUE)))),
  auto_unbox = TRUE)), error = function(e) conditionMessage(e))
cat(circe_message, "\n")
```

Do not delete criteria merely to make an import run. Translate them with a
supported native definition, verify the same population semantics, and retain
the original JSON alongside the reviewed recipe.

## Unsupported settings must remain visible

Typed constructors reject irrelevant settings instead of guessing their meaning.
For example, square-root transformation has no affine multiplier parameter:

```{r setting-refusal}
setting_message <- tryCatch(omop_number_transform("sqrt", factor = 2),
  error = function(e) conditionMessage(e))
cat(setting_message, "\n")
```

Declare an explicit affine stage and a square-root stage if that ordered
calculation is intended. A successful parse is not evidence that a statistical
option was honoured; keep execution capabilities and the explain output with
the protocol. Audit-specific catalogue settings depend on the separately
integrated audit implementation; this book does not silently install it over A-29.

The per-table codebook and its new fields belong to the categorical/layout
extension, excluded from this update. The existing dictionary in the output
chapter remains vocabulary metadata, not an invented codebook contract.

## Recap

The editable source executes once; the generated website is offline. Migration changes the recipe definitions, not just column names.

**Next:** [18. Capstone: a verbal-autopsy wide table](book-18-verbal-autopsy.html).
